so we're tracking a whole bunch of  vaccine trials and development for  coronavirus vaccines and there's news  from a couple of different vaccine  manufacturers or researchers that I  think is relevant important and  potentially optimistic which think oh we  could use a little bit of right now so  the first one is Oxford University  Oxford University could have a vaccine  ready to be used in September the reason  that Oxford has a head start is that  they've already been working on a  vaccine for prior coronaviruses and they  already have proven the the formula so  to speak to be harmless to humans from a  safety perspective so Oxford already has  a vaccine for this specific coronavirus  last month in a lab in Montana they  tested this specific vaccine on rhesus  monkeys they give the monkeys the  vaccine they exposed the monkeys to huge  amounts of the virus  the monkeys without the vaccine all got  the virus and more than 28 days later  the six monkeys that had been vaccinated  did not get the virus at any point in  time that's huge the rhesus monkey is  the closest thing to Homo sapiens for  the purposes of testing vaccines you can  read a lot about that if that's  interesting to you now is it guaranteed  that a vaccine that is safe in humans  and works in rhesus monkeys will work  just as well to create immunity in  humans no it's not a guarantee but it's  a very good sign  next step is human trials of this  specific vaccine they're going to be  doing a trial with 6,000 people by the  end of May that's very very big and will  be really important to see how that goes  now the question is does it work to  prevent corona virus in humans that's  what this 6,000 person test is meant to  figure out and a few important things  here to think about number one okay how  does a vaccine work the vaccine contains  an element of the germ that makes people  sick it could be a killed or  a conversion of the germ so that the  vaccine doesn't give you the disease  that would be a disaster some vaccines  have just part of the germ  it's enough of the germ the idea is  whether it's been weakened or it's only  a part of it or it's dead germ  it's got to be enough that the body  reacts by making antibodies which then  in an ideal world makes you immune to  the full germ if you were to be invaded  by it and you have to go through  numerous steps including testing the  vaccines for safety testing the vaccine  for efficacy seeing if it generates what  is the right dose to generate enough  antibodies that you will be immune you  can have a situation where the vaccine  is safe in the sense that it doesn't  harm humans but it might not make you  immune there can be cases where the  vaccine could make you have a more  serious version of the disease if you  were to be infected that would be a  disaster of course so what needs to  happen for this vaccine to be usable in  September first you've got to test the  6,000 humans in May that has to go well  number two there often is a production  issue when it comes to vaccines because  if you wait until you know it's the  vaccine to use to start production  you're behind and you've lost a really  important time that could be used to  start vaccinating people so what they're  going to do is now or soon they will  start production of this vaccine before  they even know for sure that it's going  to be the vaccine this is to be able to  have at least some millions of doses  ready as soon as it's determined that  this is a vaccine that works and then  lastly important if it were to be  available in September it's not going to  be everybody and their mother goes and  gets this vaccine in September if  everything goes perfectly in September  there would be a few million doses it  would probably be authorized under  emergency use and probably at that stage  it would just be for health care health  care workers which makes sense and then  subsequently for the most vulnerable  people in populations so this is all  good news but most likely there will be  more than one  vaccine that's used when things get  going because of the number of doses  globally that are going to be needed so  a couple of questions that now come up  will this be like a flu vaccine that's  only effective against like that year's  strain or mutation answer we don't know  there's a research paper that says that  the vaccine candidates for this corona  virus would match all circulating  strains of the virus but not that they  would work on all corona viruses that's  an important distinction there would be  of the vaccines being worked on now we  already have at least moderately  different strains of this corona virus  that have spread due to mutation the  vaccine would likely work on all of  those but that would not work on any  corona virus as you know there are many  corona viruses including common colds  can sometimes be caused by corona  viruses the good news is that kovat 19  seems more stable than the flu virus the  mutation rate is much lower  that helps hopefully have one that will  work longer than the flu vaccine works  it's all speculation we just don't know  at this point one other bit of hopeful  vaccine news so we this is the Oxford  trial I've been telling you about the  there's a German company working with  Pfizer which has also started human  trials and they could potentially have  millions of vaccines ready in the 2020  calendar year now that could be November  it could be the end of December but they  are starting tests in the US next week  the idea is emergency use on the table  in the fall and if they get moving on  production soon before they even know  for sure that it'll work could have a  few million doses available  they say that twelve participants have  been vaccinated since the start of the  study on April 23rd no results yet many  things happening one thing I'm going to  address at the end of the show in the  voicemail segment is whether I would  want to be at the front of the line for  these vaccines I'll address that in a  little bit today's program is sponsored  in part by the galaxies series which is  a series of three  books written by a really great author I  thought and I would call the genre sort  of political science fiction I've really  enjoyed the books it looks at the future  it explores what could happen to Planet  Earth if we stay on our path  environmentally it deals with space  travel it takes a sort of deep dive into  political psychology and it's a really  intriguing sort of dystopian look at the  future the stories are told from a  perspective that I think people in the  audience would really appreciate and  there's a ton of really excellent  artwork also that accompanies the books  I read a lot of books in this genre and  I'll tell you these books are very well  written the plot the characters are  compelling if you're someone who likes  politics and sci-fi you'll really like  these books again it's the galaxy series  check it out for yourself for a limited  time we have a special deal set up where  my audience can get each book for just  99 cents go to david pakman.com slash  galaxy
 

